Leave the cursor out of the picture and tell the application what it looks like, so that
whoever shows the picture somewhere else can draw the cursor itself, where its own pointer is.
Off unless asked for.

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86, on top of gst-wayland-display-render-on-commit.patch in this
directory. The lines of context are from that project and from that patch.

It is kept small and in a shape that can be proposed to that project
(docs/adr/0009-host-compositor.md): the element `waylanddisplaysrc` gets a property,
`cursor-messages`, that is off by default, and with it off nothing changes. The rules are in
one new file with its tests, wayland-display-core/src/comp/cursor.rs, which also says why.

What the mode does:

- The cursor is not drawn into the frames. Moving the pointer then no longer changes the
  picture, and in the "render on commit" mode no longer makes a frame go out.
- Whenever the cursor changes, the element posts an application message on its bus, as it
  does for its environment (`wayland.src`). The structure is called `wayland.cursor` and has
  a `kind`:
    "hidden"   the client under the pointer has hidden it
    "named"    a cursor there is no image of, with its `name` ("default" before any client
               has set one, and for an image that cannot be read)
    "image"    with `width`, `height`, `hotspot-x`, `hotspot-y` (in pixels of the image),
               `scale` (pixels of the image to one of the picture) and `image`, a buffer of
               four bytes a pixel: red, green, blue, alpha, the colours multiplied by alpha
  The first message comes when the property is set, with the cursor as it is then. A cursor
  that is set again without having changed is not told again.
- The image comes from a client and is treated as untrusted: it is read only from a
  shared-memory buffer in one of the two formats every client has (ARGB8888, XRGB8888), only
  when offset, stride and size fit the buffer's real length, only up to 256 pixels a side, and
  only with its hot spot inside it. Anything else is told as "named", "default".
- The core has `WaylandDisplay::set_cursor_sink` for callers that do not go through the
  element.

Tests (`cargo test -p wayland-display-core`): seven of the rules for reading an image and
telling a change, and seven with a real Wayland client that sets a cursor, changes it, hides
it, hands in one that is too large, and whose frames are looked at with and without the mode.

Known limits:

- A cursor whose image is a buffer on the GPU is told without its image.
- The image is copied and compared on every commit to the cursor's surface. An animated
  cursor makes a message per step.
- Nothing is exposed through the C bindings yet.

Apply and build, in a checkout that has the first patch applied:

    git apply <this file>
    cargo build --release -p gst-plugin-wayland-display -p wayland-display-core --features cuda

